    <artifactId>vidulum-cashflow-tests</artifactId>
    <name>Vidulum CashFlow Cross-Module Tests</name>
    <description>
        No src/main. The name says what this module is: the tests that need a running system, for
        the five artifacts the cashflow family was split into.

        It exists for the same reason vidulum-wealth-tests does. After bank-data-ingestion,
        bank-data-adapter and user-financial-profile became their own artifacts, their integration
        tests could not follow them - each of those modules depends on contracts only, never on
        vidulum-cashflow, and that is exactly what keeps the reactor acyclic. So the tests stayed
        behind, and vidulum-cashflow ended up being two things at once: the aggregate's
        implementation, and the place everyone else's integration tests lived. It had four
        test-scope dependencies on modules that had left it.

        Now it has none, and keeps only tests that need no Spring and no Docker. Everything that
        needs a context, a Mongo or a Kafka lives here.

        It also owns CashFlowTestApplication, and that is not incidental: @SpringBootTest finds an
        application by scanning upwards from the test's own package, so the application has to sit
        in com.multi.vidulum next to the tests that need it.
    </description>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- Contracts -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.multi</groupId>
            <artifactId>vidulum-shared-kernel</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.multi</groupId>
            <artifactId>vidulum-cashflow-api</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.multi</groupId>
            <artifactId>vidulum-user-financial-profile-api</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Every implementation in the family, at test scope: that is the whole point of this
             module. None of them may depend on each other this way, and none has to. -->
